# Mock Assessment: Book Manager API

**Time limit: 40 minutes. Set a timer before you start.**
AI tools and Google are allowed. You must be able to explain every line afterwards.

## Task
Build a REST API for managing books, using Django and Django REST Framework.

## Setup (do this yourself, no copy-paste of old code)
1. Create a new folder `book_manager`, open it in VS Code.
2. Create a virtual environment and install: django, djangorestframework, django-cors-headers.
3. Create the project `config` and an app `books`.
4. Use SQLite.

## Requirements

### 1. Model `Book`
| Field | Type | Rules |
|---|---|---|
| title | text, max 150 | required |
| author | text, max 100 | required |
| isbn | text, max 13 | required, unique |
| price | decimal | must be greater than 0 |
| published_year | integer | between 1900 and 2026 |
| available | boolean | default True |
| created_at | datetime | set automatically |

### 2. Endpoints (all under `/api/`)
- `GET /api/books/` list all books (newest first)
- `POST /api/books/` create a book
- `GET /api/books/<id>/` get one book
- `PUT/PATCH /api/books/<id>/` update a book
- `DELETE /api/books/<id>/` delete a book

### 3. Features
- Search by title or author: `/api/books/?search=python`
- Custom endpoint: `GET /api/books/available/` returns only books where available is True
- Validation errors must return status 400 with a clear message

### 4. Frontend (do this if time remains)
Create `index.html` that:
- shows all books in a table (use `fetch()`)
- has a form to add a new book
- has a Delete button on each row

### 5. Submission
- Add a `.gitignore` (venv, db.sqlite3, __pycache__)
- Add a short `README.md` explaining how to run the project
- Commit and push to a new GitHub repository named `book-manager`

## Final check (last 4 minutes)
- [ ] `python manage.py makemigrations` and `migrate` run with no errors
- [ ] `python manage.py runserver` starts cleanly
- [ ] Every endpoint above works when tested in the browser
- [ ] Code is pushed to GitHub and visible online

